---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import DecisionPathBlock from "../../components/DecisionPathBlock.astro";
import EvidenceLimitsBlock from "../../components/EvidenceLimitsBlock.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import { editorialTeam } from "../../data/editorial";
import { cx30GuideQuality } from "../../data/mazda-cx30-quality";

const title = "2020 Mazda CX-30: what to avoid";
const description = "Used-buyer red flags for a 2020 Mazda CX-30: missing VIN evidence, unresolved safety symptoms, unsupported suspension diagnoses, and incomplete maintenance records.";
const image = "/images/photos/cars/mazda-cx-30-2020.webp";
const modifiedTime = new Date("2026-09-01");
const quality = cx30GuideQuality["mazda-cx-30-2020-what-to-avoid"];
const breadcrumbs = [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Guides", href: "/guides/" }, { label: title }];
const related = [
  { href: "/guides/mazda-cx-30-2020-what-to-check-before-buying/", title: "CX-30 used-buyer inspection", description: "Work through records, condition, and road-test checks before deciding whether to negotiate or walk away.", eyebrow: "Buying used" },
  { href: "/problems/front-end-clunk-over-bumps-mazda-cx-30-2020/", title: "CX-30 front rattle diagnosis", description: "A symptom-led diagnosis that stays within Mazda's documented service scope.", eyebrow: "Diagnosis" },
  { href: "/checklists/used-car-viewing/", title: "Printable used-car viewing worksheet", description: "Write down the evidence, uncertainties, and repair questions while inspecting the car.", eyebrow: "Worksheet" }
];
---

<ContentLayout title={title} description={description} intro="Avoid paying for a clean appearance while records, VIN checks, tire condition, braking behaviour, or a front-suspension complaint remain unexplained. The decision should follow evidence on the exact vehicle." image={image} modifiedTime={modifiedTime} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} editorialSummary="A used-buyer red-flag guide that distinguishes a documented maintenance reset from an unresolved safety, record, or diagnosis issue." editorialByline={`By ${editorialTeam.name}`} editorialReviewedLabel="Reviewed September 1, 2026" editorialBadges={["Used-buyer red flags", "VIN first", "Diagnosis before parts"]}>
  <EvidenceLimitsBlock {...quality.evidence} />
  <DecisionPathBlock title="Do not buy the explanation" intro="Require a VIN, a coherent record, and an inspection path for symptoms that affect safety or the price." steps={quality.decisionPath} />

  <Section title="Walk away or pause the sale" intro="These conditions make the risk too uncertain without independent inspection or better evidence.">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">The seller will not share the VIN, the title history conflicts with the vehicle, or the service record is missing while the price assumes a fully maintained car.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Brake, steering, stability, or warning-light symptoms are unresolved or minimised. These are inspection issues, not negotiation details to accept on trust.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">A front rattle has been diagnosed remotely as a link without an invoice, a confirmed failed part, or consideration of Mazda's mount and damper paths.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">The seller refuses a pre-purchase inspection after the road test or paperwork raises a reasonable question.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Avoid expensive assumptions" intro="The same symptom can lead to different work, and the same model year can have different build and service histories.">
    <p>Do not assume every 2020 CX-30 falls within a recall or technical bulletin. Use the VIN for recall status, production information where a bulletin specifies it, and the actual repair history for the vehicle. A published document can define a technician's decision path without proving that the condition is present, common, or responsible for every similar noise.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Avoid treating a retailer listing as fitment confirmation. For parts, confirm the VIN, current part number, exact axle position, quantity, and return terms after a qualified diagnosis. A listed price does not establish the installed repair cost.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Check claims against documents" intro="A clear answer lets you decide whether the price reflects a completed job, a known cost, or an unresolved risk.">
    <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3"><strong>Recall claim:</strong> Run the VIN through the official recall lookup and compare it with the seller's paperwork. Mazda's 2020 <a class="font-semibold text-accent hover:text-ink" href="https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2020/RCLRPT-20V346-7731.PDF" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">seat-rail recall report</a> describes its affected population; it is not proof that a particular vehicle has been repaired.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3"><strong>Repair claim:</strong> Ask for the invoice, mileage, repair date, fault description, and part number. “Fixed last year” is not enough to decide whether the original issue was diagnosed, repaired, or merely quiet for a period.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3"><strong>Noise claim:</strong> Reproduce the symptom on a route that includes the conditions described by the seller. A noise that cannot be reproduced can still justify an inspection, but it should not be priced as a confirmed component failure.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3"><strong>Service claim:</strong> Compare the invoice dates and mileage with Mazda's maintenance schedule. A stamped book with no supporting work detail may establish less than an invoice that identifies fluids, filters, and inspection results.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Set a condition before negotiating" intro="A price discussion is clearer when the seller's answer changes the next action rather than simply sounding reassuring.">
    <p>Proceed only after agreeing what evidence is missing and who will obtain it. For a routine item, the condition may be an itemized quote and a written allowance in the price. For a safety-related symptom, a warning light, or an unresolved steering or braking concern, the appropriate condition is an independent inspection or walking away. Keep the agreed points on the viewing worksheet so the final decision is based on the vehicle inspected, not on later recollection.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">These checks do not diagnose a fault remotely. They identify questions for a used-car decision; a qualified workshop should assess any safety-related or persistent mechanical concern.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="A manageable maintenance reset" intro="These issues can be reasonable when they are documented, priced transparently, and separate from unresolved safety symptoms.">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-steel">
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">Routine maintenance due under Mazda's schedule, with a record that lets a workshop establish the next correct interval.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">A known tire, brake, battery, or filter expense supported by a condition check and an itemized quote.</li>
      <li class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">A completed and documented repair where the symptom no longer reproduces and the invoice identifies the work performed.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <RelatedLinks title="CX-30 buying resources" intro="Use these before paying for an individual car or selecting a part." items={related} />
</ContentLayout>
